WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 13: The Exploding Whale of Oregon: A Highway Division's Blubber Blast

1
00:00:02.480 --> 00:00:04.960
[Rebecca] Welcome to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:04.960 --> 00:00:06.720
[Rebecca] I'm your host, Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.720 --> 00:00:15.120
[Rebecca] Sometimes, true history is far stranger, more chaotic, and more hilariously unbelievable than any fiction a comedy writer could ever invent.

4
00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:21.040
[Rebecca] Today, we travel to the rugged, misty coast of the Pacific Northwest in the autumn of 1970.

5
00:00:21.040 --> 00:00:29.920
[Rebecca] When an 8-ton, 45-foot rotting sperm whale washed ashore on an Oregon beach, local authorities faced a pungent public health crisis.

6
00:00:29.920 --> 00:00:40.400
[Rebecca] Due to an unusual loophole in state law, jurisdiction over the dead marine mammal fell not to wildlife officials or marine biologists, but to the Oregon State Highway Division.

7
00:00:40.400 --> 00:00:42.640
[Rebecca] Their official engineering solution?

8
00:00:42.640 --> 00:00:49.120
[Rebecca] Pack 20 cases of high explosives under the blubber, light the fuse, and blast the carcass into oblivion.

9
00:00:49.120 --> 00:00:54.720
[Rebecca] What followed was the most bizarre, blubber-drenched municipal disaster in American history.

10
00:00:54.720 --> 00:00:58.000
[Rebecca] Here is George with the story.

11
00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:09.040
[George] On the chilly, overcast morning of November 9, 1970, residents of the quiet coastal town of Florence, Oregon discovered a monumental arrival on the sand.

12
00:01:09.040 --> 00:01:19.440
[George] A massive male sperm whale, measuring 45 feet in length and weighing over eight tons, had died at sea and drifted onto the surf line near the South Jetty.

13
00:01:19.440 --> 00:01:28.960
[George] Sperm whales are among the largest carnivores on Earth, equipped with blubber layers up to 12 inches thick to insulate against the crushing cold of the ocean abyss.

14
00:01:28.960 --> 00:01:36.000
[George] Within 48 hours, under the coastal breeze and direct sunlight, the dead Leviathan began to decompose.

15
00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:49.280
[George] Internal anaerobic bacteria multiplied rapidly, producing massive volumes of methane, hydrogen sulfide, and cadaverine gases that inflated the whale's belly like a gigantic, putrid black balloon.

16
00:01:49.280 --> 00:02:00.040
[George] The stench was indescribable, a sickening, sweet, eye-watering rot that wafted miles inland into neighborhood living rooms, school hallways, and grocery stores.

17
00:01:59.840 --> 00:02:04.120
[George] Local municipal and lane county officials were completely paralyzed.

18
00:02:04.360 --> 00:02:16.840
[George] The whale was far too massive to drag back out to sea, as local commercial tugboat captains flatly refused to enter the treacherous shallow breakers and risk grounding their vessels in the pounding Pacific Surf.

19
00:02:16.840 --> 00:02:30.680
[George] It was too enormous to bury on site with backhoes, because marine geologists and oceanographers warned that winter king tides and gale force Pacific storms would quickly wash the beach sand away, uncovering the decaying carcass.

20
00:02:30.680 --> 00:02:33.480
[George] And then came the jurisdictional absurdity.

21
00:02:33.480 --> 00:02:44.520
[George] Under an eccentric 1913 Oregon statute championed by Governor Oswald West, all Pacific Ocean beaches in the state were officially classified as public state highways.

22
00:02:44.520 --> 00:02:58.200
[George] Therefore, legal jurisdiction over the eight-ton dead marine mammal fell not to the Department of Fish and Wildlife, the County Sheriff, or the Coast Guard, but squarely upon the shoulders of the Oregon State Highway Division.

23
00:02:58.200 --> 00:03:08.520
[George] The unenviable task of disposing of the rotting behemoth was assigned to George Thornton, a veteran assistant district engineer for the Oregon Department of Transportation.

24
00:03:08.520 --> 00:03:19.800
[George] Thornton was a practical, confident road builder who had spent his career blasting highway road cuts through solid basalt and granite cliffs in the Cascade Mountains with commercial explosives.

25
00:03:19.800 --> 00:03:33.080
[George] Faced with an eight-ton mountain of decaying whale flesh, Thornton consulted with munitions experts from the United States Navy and local commercial explosive contractors, arriving at an audacious and fateful decision.

26
00:03:33.080 --> 00:03:46.400
[George] Instead of cutting the rotting carcass into manageable pieces with chainsaws, meat hooks, and flencing knives, a nauseating, stomach-turning job that no highway worker was willing to perform, they would use dynamite.

27
00:03:44.440 --> 00:03:55.920
[George] Thornton reasoned that if they buried 20 wooden cases of commercial dynamite, amounting to half a ton of high explosives, beneath the landward side of the whale.

28
00:03:55.920 --> 00:04:04.240
[George] The directional force of the blast would obliterate the carcass into microscopic particles while propelling the remaining bulk out into the Pacific surf.

29
00:04:04.240 --> 00:04:11.520
[George] Thornton calculated that the explosive force would lift the bulk seaward, relying on the sea breeze to disperse the smoke.

30
00:04:11.520 --> 00:04:26.480
[George] There, as Thornton cheerfully assured local television news reporters, the thousands of hungry seagulls living along the coast would swoop down and consume every scrap of blubber, leaving the pristine Oregon beach completely clean within 24 hours.

31
00:04:26.480 --> 00:04:35.840
[George] On the morning of Thursday, November 12, 1970, an air of festive, carnival-like excitement descended upon the beaches of Florence.

32
00:04:35.840 --> 00:04:49.600
[George] Over 75 spectators, including families with young children, picnickers, local dignitaries, and television camera crews, gathered on the grassy coastal dunes a quarter mile away to witness the grand detonation.

33
00:04:49.600 --> 00:04:59.360
[George] Among the spectators was Walter Humanhofer, a savvy military veteran who had received specialized training in heavy explosives while serving in the armed forces.

34
00:04:59.360 --> 00:05:13.200
[George] Humanhofer was in Florence on business, driving a brand new, luxurious Oldsmobile 98 Regency sedan that he had purchased just days earlier during a promotional sale with the ironic slogan, a whale of a deal.

35
00:05:13.200 --> 00:05:22.720
[George] When Humanhofer observed the highway division workers unloading 20 full cases of commercial dynamite from their pickup trucks, he was utterly horrified.

36
00:05:22.720 --> 00:05:39.720
[George] He approached George Thornton and warned him urgently that twenty cases of dynamite was completely insane, twenty sticks would be sufficient to nudge the whale into the ocean surf, whereas half a ton would act like a massive military surface mine, throwing heavy debris for thousands of yards.

37
00:05:39.720 --> 00:05:50.440
[George] Humanhofer explained that blubber was soft and elastic, meaning it would absorb the blast shockwave and break into enormous airborne projectiles rather than vaporizing into dust.

38
00:05:50.440 --> 00:05:59.960
[George] Thornton dismissed the veteran's warning with bureaucratic disdain, stating that the highway department understood explosives and knew precisely what it was doing.

39
00:05:59.960 --> 00:06:08.200
[George] Humanhofer shook his head, walked back to his brand new Oldsmobile parked a quartermile away in the gravel lot, and hoped for the best.

40
00:06:08.200 --> 00:06:19.240
[George] At precisely 11:45 in the morning, highway patrolman cleared the immediate beach and pushed all spectators back to the safety perimeter on the bluff, 400 yards away.

41
00:06:19.240 --> 00:06:36.440
[George] Paul Lindman, a young television reporter for KATU Channel 2 News in Portland, stood before the camera holding his microphone alongside his cameraman Doug Brazil, introducing what he promised his viewers would be a triumphant demonstration of American state highway engineering.

42
00:06:36.440 --> 00:06:40.600
[George] The highway engineer connected the blasting wire to the battery box.

43
00:06:40.600 --> 00:06:43.800
[George] The countdown echoed through the salty sea breeze.

44
00:06:43.800 --> 00:06:45.400
[George] 3-2-1.

45
00:06:45.400 --> 00:06:47.320
[George] The plunger was shoved down.

46
00:06:47.320 --> 00:06:52.920
[George] The beach detonated with an earth-shattering roar that shook the coastal bluffs like a massive earthquake.

47
00:06:52.920 --> 00:07:04.120
[George] A colossal apocalyptic geyser of black sand, white foam, and red whale blubber erupted into the air, soaring over 800 feet into the sky like a volcanic eruption.

48
00:07:04.120 --> 00:07:09.160
[George] The shockwave knocked hats off spectators and rattled car windows half a mile away.

49
00:07:09.160 --> 00:07:15.920
[George] For two brief seconds, the crowd cheered in awe and delight at the spectacular visual magnitude of the blast.

50
00:07:15.920 --> 00:07:18.640
[George] And then, everyone looked up at the sky.

51
00:07:18.640 --> 00:07:25.120
[George] As the black smoke cleared, the joyful cheers turned into shrieks of sheer, unadulterated terror.

52
00:07:25.120 --> 00:07:27.280
[George] The sky was raining blubber.

53
00:07:27.280 --> 00:07:38.480
[George] Giant, greasy, putrid chunks of rotting whale fat, ranging in size from baseballs to 60-pound boulders, plummeted from the clouds directly onto the fleeing spectators.

54
00:07:38.480 --> 00:07:50.320
[George] People screamed, covering their heads and diving under picnic tables and sand dunes as decomposing blubber rained down like mortar shells, spattering coats, hair, and camera equipment.

55
00:07:50.320 --> 00:08:03.600
[George] Four hundred yards away in the visitor parking lot, a massive, six-foot chunk of flying whale blubber fell from the stratosphere and scored a direct hit upon the roof of Walter Humanhoefer's brand new Oldsmobile sedan.

56
00:08:03.600 --> 00:08:05.600
[George] The impact was devastating.

57
00:08:05.600 --> 00:08:16.000
[George] The steel roof was caved down to the level of the passenger seats, the safety glass windows were shattered outward, and the luxury sedan was flattened like a crushed tin can.

58
00:08:16.000 --> 00:08:28.000
[George] Miraculously, no human beings were killed or seriously injured, although dozens of spectators were drenched from head to toe in foul, nauseating whale slime that stained clothing permanently.

59
00:08:28.000 --> 00:08:34.880
[George] When the shocked survivors picked themselves up and looked back at the beach, they were greeted by two final absurdities.

60
00:08:34.880 --> 00:08:44.640
[George] First, the tremendous explosion had terrified every seagull within twenty miles, sending them fleeing inland, with not a single bird returning to scavenge.

61
00:08:44.640 --> 00:08:58.720
[George] And second, sitting stubbornly on the sand, entirely unmoved by half a ton of dynamite, was a massive remaining chunk of the whale carcass, which the exhausted highway crew was ultimately forced to bury by hand with bulldozers.

62
00:08:58.720 --> 00:09:06.280
[George] Paul Lindman's television broadcast for KATU news became an instant masterpiece of American broadcast journalism.

63
00:09:06.280 --> 00:09:19.400
[George] His deadpan narration of the fiasco, immortalizing the famous line that the blast blasted blubber beyond all believable bounds, was aired across the Pacific Northwest and quickly passed into regional folklore.

64
00:09:19.400 --> 00:09:29.400
[George] For two decades, the incident was treated as an urban legend outside of Oregon, with many refusing to believe a government agency could do something so preposterous.

65
00:09:29.400 --> 00:09:39.320
[George] In 1990, syndicated humor columnist Dave Bari wrote a celebrated column about the incident, bringing it to national and international prominence.

66
00:09:39.320 --> 00:09:53.720
[George] With the advent of the world wide web, digitized video copies of the KATU news broadcast were uploaded to early Internet bulletin boards and Usenet groups, becoming one of the very first viral video sensations in internet history.

67
00:09:53.720 --> 00:09:59.880
[George] Fifty years after the blast, the city of Florence proudly embraced its bizarre claim to fame.

68
00:09:59.880 --> 00:10:09.160
[George] In 2020, residents voted overwhelmingly to name their newly renovated public recreation space, Exploding Whale Memorial Park.

69
00:10:09.160 --> 00:10:14.200
[George] The incident remains an eternal monument to American bureaucratic overconfidence.

70
00:10:14.200 --> 00:10:25.640
[George] Proving that when human engineering clashes with eight tons of decomposing sea life and twenty cases of dynamite, gravity and rotting blubber will always have the final laugh.

71
00:10:32.240 --> 00:10:41.600
[Rebecca] Fifty years later, the footage remains a viral legend, immortalizing the eternal lesson that sometimes brute force is no substitute for a bulldozer.

72
00:10:41.560 --> 00:10:44.560
[Rebecca] Thank you for listening to most fascinating stories.

73
00:10:44.560 --> 00:10:45.760
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

74
00:10:45.760 --> 00:10:49.120
[Rebecca] Join us next time for another forgotten chapter of history.

75
00:10:44.200 --> 00:10:51.200
[Rebecca] Until then, stay curious.

